# COSMIC RAYS IN THE INTERPLANETARY MEDIUM L. J. GLEESON AND W. I. AXFORD\* Cornell–Sydney University Astronomy Center, Cornell University, Ithaca, New York Received June 1, 1967 In this Letter we discuss the equations governing the behavior of cosmic-ray particles in a model of the interplanetary medium. The equations provide direct information concerning the streaming and spatial gradient of cosmic rays and when combined reduce to the Fokker-Planck equation used in this context by Parker (1965, 1966). It is shown that in suitable conditions the cosmic rays behave as if they were being modulated by a heliocentric electric field, although this can only be an approximate description of the over-all modulation. We consider a spherically symmetric configuration in which the solar wind carries magnetic irregularities radially with speed $V(\boldsymbol{r})$ . The magnetic irregularities are assumed to act as perfectly elastic scatterers of cosmic rays in a frame of reference moving with the solar wind, and to have a number density $N(\boldsymbol{r})$ and scattering cross-section $\sigma(v)$ , where v is the velocity of a cosmic-ray particle. The configuration can include a radial magnetic field, but this does not affect the results. The Boltzmann equation appropriate to this situation is $$
\left[ \frac {\partial}{\partial t} + v \cos \Theta \frac {\partial}{\partial r} - \frac {v}{r} \sin \Theta \frac {\partial}{\partial \Theta} \right] f (r, v, \Theta , t) = \left(\frac {\delta f}{\delta t}\right) _ {c}, \tag {1}
$$ where $\Theta$ is the angle between v and r and $(\delta f/\delta t)_{c}$ represents the effects of scattering. We evaluate $(\delta f/\delta t)_{c}$ first in the frame which moves with the scatterers, and make a relativistic transformation of the result back to the fixed frame (L. J. Gleeson, in preparation). Thus, for isotropic scattering $$
\left(\frac {\delta f}{\delta t}\right) _ {c} = - 4 \pi N (r) \sigma (v _ {m}) v _ {m} \left(1 - \frac {V v \cos \Theta}{c ^ {2}}\right) f (r, v, \Theta , t)
$$ $$
+ \frac {2 \pi N (r) v _ {m}}{(1 - V v \cos \Theta / c ^ {2}) ^ {4}} \int_ {0} ^ {\pi} \sigma (v _ {m}) \left(1 - \frac {v ^ {\prime} V \cos \Theta^ {\prime}}{c ^ {2}}\right) ^ {5} f (r, v ^ {\prime}, \Theta^ {\prime}, t) \sin \Theta_ {m} ^ {\prime} d \Theta_ {m} ^ {\prime},
$$ where the subscript $m$ refers to the moving frame and primes to the particle velocity before scattering. The first and second terms in equation (2) represent scattering out of and into a given element of phase space, respectively. It is assumed that the distribution function can be written as a Legendre expansion as follows: $$
f (r, v, \Theta , t) = \sum_ {i = 0} ^ {\infty} f _ {i} (r, v, t) P _ {i} (\cos \Theta). \tag {3}
$$ We substitute this into equations (1) and (2), make a Taylor expansion of $(\delta f/\delta t)_{c}$ in powers of $(V/v)$ , and assume near-isotropy so that $f_{i} \ll f_{0}, f_{1}(i \geq 2)$ . Note that $V/v \approx 10^{-2}$ for 10 MeV protons, and that the observed deviations from isotropy are small \* Present address: Departments of Physics and Applied Electrophysics, University of California, San Diego, La Jolla, California. (McCracken 1966). Retaining terms necessary to determine $f_0$ and $f_1$ to $O(Vf / v)$ then, from the coefficients of $P_0$ and $P_1$ , we obtain $$
\begin{array}{l} 3 \frac {\partial f _ {0}}{\partial t} + \frac {1}{r ^ {2}} \frac {\partial}{\partial r} (r ^ {2} v f _ {1}) = \frac {4 \pi N V}{v ^ {2}} \frac {\partial}{\partial v} [ \sigma v ^ {3} (1 - v ^ {2} / c ^ {2}) f _ {1} ] (4) \\ \left. + \frac {4 \pi N V ^ {2}}{v ^ {2}} \frac {\partial}{\partial v} \left\{\frac {\sigma v ^ {3}}{\left(1 - v ^ {2} / c ^ {2}\right) ^ {1 / 2}} \frac {\partial}{\partial v} \left[ \left(1 - \frac {v ^ {2}}{c ^ {2}}\right) ^ {5 / 2} f _ {0} \right] \right\}, \right. \\ \frac {\partial f _ {1}}{\partial t} + v \frac {\partial f _ {0}}{\partial r} = - 4 \pi N \sigma v f _ {1} - \frac {4 \pi N \sigma V v}{(1 - v ^ {2} / c ^ {2}) ^ {3 / 2}} \frac {\partial}{\partial v} [ (1 - v ^ {2} / c ^ {2}) ^ {5 / 2} f _ {0} ]. (5) \\ \end{array}
$$ It has been assumed that the distribution function is sufficiently smooth for $f$ , $v\partial f / \partial v$ , and $v^{2}\partial^{2}f / \partial v^{2}$ to be considered of the same order of magnitude. Care must be taken (especially in the coefficient of $P_0$ ) to insure that all significant terms are included. Since $\partial f / \partial r \sim (4\pi N\sigma V / v)f$ and $f_{1}$ is roughly of order $(Vf_0 / v)$ , it is necessary to include terms involving $V^{2}f_{0}$ in equation (4); the terms which have been neglected are of order $(V^{3}f_{0} / v^{3})$ in equation (4) and $(V^{2}f_{0} / v^{2})$ in equation (5). In general $$
