# 2. THE TIME-DEPENDENT DRIFT MODEL The model is based on the standard cosmic-ray transport equation (Parker 1965) which for the distribution function $f(\boldsymbol{r}, P, t)$ of cosmic rays with rigidity $P$ , position $\boldsymbol{r}$ , and time $t$ , is $$
\frac {\partial f}{\partial t} = - \left(\boldsymbol {V} + \langle \boldsymbol {v} _ {D} \rangle\right) \cdot \boldsymbol {\nabla} f + \boldsymbol {\nabla} \cdot \left(\kappa^ {(s)} \cdot \boldsymbol {\nabla} f\right) + \frac {1}{3} (\boldsymbol {\nabla} \cdot \boldsymbol {V}) \frac {\partial f}{\partial \ln P}. \tag {1}
$$ The terms on the right-hand side represent convection, drift, diffusion, and adiabatic energy changes, respectively; $V$ is the solar wind velocity, $\langle \boldsymbol{v}_D \rangle = \nabla \times \kappa_T \boldsymbol{e}_B$ is the averaged guiding center gradient and curvature drift velocity in the large-scale IMF, $\kappa_T$ (the drift coefficient) is the antisymmetric part and $\kappa^{(s)}$ the symmetric part of the diffusion tensor. Using a heliocentric spherical coordinate system, and assuming azimuthal symmetry, equation (1) was solved numerically for $f(r, \theta, P, t)$ with $r$ the radial distance and $\theta$ the polar angle, to find the differential intensity $j_E \propto P^2 f$ ( $E$ is kinetic energy). The alternating direction implicit (ADI) finite difference method, first used by Fisk (1976) for modulation studies, but adapted to handle time dependence (le Roux 1990), was employed for this purpose. The background radially directed solar wind speed was specified to increase with latitude according to $V = 400(1 + \cos^{2} \theta)$ , when $45^{\circ} \leq \theta \leq 90^{\circ}$ , with V = 600 km s $^{-1}$ when $\theta < 45^{\circ}$ . The symmetric part of the diffusion tensor consists of the diffusion coefficients parallel ( $\kappa_{\parallel}$ ) and perpendicular ( $\kappa_{\perp}$ ) to the mean IMF direction, which can be combined to give the effective radial diffusion coefficient: $$
\kappa_ {r r} = \kappa_ {| |} \cos^ {2} \psi + \kappa_ {\perp} \sin^ {2} \psi , \tag {2}
$$ with $\psi$ the spiral angle of the IMF and $\tan \psi = \Omega r \sin \theta / V$ ( $\Omega$ the rotation speed of the Sun) in accordance with the Parker spiral field model. The diffusion coefficients were defined as $$
\kappa_ {\parallel} = \kappa_ {0} \beta \bigg (\frac {P}{P _ {0}} \bigg) \bigg (\frac {B _ {0}}{3 B _ {p} (r , \theta)} \bigg) \bigg (\frac {B _ {0}}{B _ {t} (t)} \bigg) ;
$$ $$
\kappa_ {\perp} = \kappa_ {\perp o} \kappa_ {\parallel}. \tag {3}
$$ The constant $\kappa_0 = 2 \times 10^{22} \, \mathrm{cm}^2 \, \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $\beta$ is the ratio of the particle velocity to the velocity of light. The normalization factor $P_0 = 1 \, \mathrm{GV}$ , while the ratio $P/P_0$ is fixed at 0.4 when $P < 0.4 \, \mathrm{GV}$ . In this way it is ensured that the parallel mean path $\lambda_{\parallel}$ adheres to the Palmer consensus values (Palmer 1982) for $P \leq 1 \, \mathrm{GV}$ if $\kappa_{\parallel} = v\lambda_{\parallel}/3$ ; $B_\rho(r, \theta)$ is the large-scale IMF magnitude according to the Parker spiral field model, thereby supplying the background spatial dependence of the diffusion coefficients. Also, $B_p$ is normalized to $B_0 = 0.4$ nT, the assumed background IMF magnitude at 10 AU in the equatorial plane. This assumed inverse relationship between the diffusion coefficient and $B_p$ has some theoretical justification because it ensures that the assumption of weak scattering ( $\lambda_{\parallel}/r_g \gg 1$ , with $r_g$ the cosmic-ray gyroradius) is upheld throughout the heliosphere (Jokipii & Davilla 1981). It also provides an acceptable background spatial dependence ( $\propto r$ ) in and around the equatorial plane in the outer heliosphere (McDonald et al. 1992). In the heliosphere polar regions it was assumed that the IMF magnitude is modified by the presence of transverse turbulence as explained by Jokipii & Kóta (1989). For the unmodified field $B_p(\theta = 90^\circ)/B_p(\theta = 0^\circ) = r$ , but for the modified field a strong deviation was assumed, $B_p(\theta = 90^\circ)/B_p(\theta = 0^\circ) = 2$ , in order to study maximum effects on cosmic-ray transport. The time dependence of the coefficients for a given $(r, \theta)$ is determined by $B_t$ . Within this context a MIR can be introduced into the model as an outward propagating region of enhanced IMF magnitude relative to the background IMF ( $B_0/B_t < 1$ ), causing a localized decrease of the diffusion coefficients. The MIR therefore acts as a propagating diffusion barrier toward inflowing cosmic rays. A rarefaction region (RR) is defined as a propagating region where $B_0/B_t > 1$ and gives relatively little resistance to the cosmic-ray flow. This allows for cosmic-ray modulation due to changing combinations of MIRs and RRs with time, as shown empirically (Burlaga et al. 1985) and with modulation models (Perko & Burlaga 1992; Potgieter et al. 1993). In the absence of MIRs and RRs, $B_0/B_t = 1$ , and the diffusion coefficients assume their steady state background values. The constant $\kappa_{\perp 0}$ was fixed at 0.01 which ensured that the ratio of $\kappa_{\perp}/\kappa_{\parallel} \ll 1$ , throughout the heliosphere, in accordance with the prediction from kinetic theory for an isotropic scattering model in the weak scattering limit (e.g., Kóta 1990) in agreement with the Palmer consensus values at Earth. Under the assumption of weak scattering, the antisymmetric part of the diffusion tensor was defined as $$
\kappa_ {T} = \kappa_ {T 0} \beta P \bigg (\frac {1}{3 B _ {p} (r , \theta)} \bigg) \bigg (\frac {B _ {0}}{B _ {t}} \bigg), \tag {4}
$$ with $\kappa_{T0}$ a constant that allows for the global scaling of drift effects. In this paper $\kappa_{T0}=1$ , which imply full drifts. The presence of $B_{0}/B_{t}$ in equation (4) means that the drift coefficient is affected in the same way as the diffusion coefficients in equation (3) by changes in the time-dependent IMF. During the passage of a MIR ( $B_{0}/B_{t}<1$ ) drift effects are reduced which implies that a MIR acts also as a drift barrier to incoming cosmic rays. This ties in with the calculations of Burger & Moraal (1990) which showed that the spatially averaged drift velocity for long-wavelength field variations is lower than for a smooth field. Instead of using the complex measured IMF magnitude as input in equations (2) and (3), we describe MIRs and RRs by modeling them as simple sinusoidal regions of enhanced or diminished magnetic fields, assuming $$
B _ {t} = \frac {B _ {0}}{1 \pm C (t , \theta) \sin^ {2} \left[ \pi (t - t _ {\mathrm{MIR}}) / (\Delta t _ {\mathrm{MIR}} (t) \right]},
$$ $$
t _ {\mathbf {M I R}} \leq t \leq (t _ {\mathbf {M I R}} + \Delta t _ {\mathbf {M I R}}). \tag {5}
$$ The minus sign signifies the passage of a MIR and the plus sign a RR. In this equation t is real time, $t_{MIR}$ is the arrival time of the MIR, and $\Delta t_{MIR}$ the time it takes a MIR to pass an obser- ver. The MIR can be expanded radially inward and outward by increasing $\Delta t_{\mathrm{MIR}}(\Delta r_{\mathrm{MIR}} = V \Delta t_{\mathrm{MIR}})$ according to the Alfvén speed in the solar wind medium (Burlaga et al. 1991). Also, $C(t, \theta)$ determines the maximum variation of $B_t$ from $B_0$ at a given position in the heliosphere and can be interpreted as the resultant relative amplitude of the change in the diffusion and drift coefficients during the passage of a MIR/RR. It is a flexible parameter in the code which can be changed with time to produce growth or decay of the MIR/RR as it propagates outward. In addition, $C(t, \theta)$ may be decreased sinusoidally with increasing latitude above the equatorial plane, thereby enabling the modeling of spherical MIRs/RRs with a limited latitudinal extent. The changes in the solar wind velocity during the passage of MIRs/RRs were ignored since test runs showed them to be of secondary importance compared to the IMF variations. This is supported by Kóta & Jokipii (1991) who studied the effects of corotating interaction regions on cosmic-ray modulation with their three-dimensional model. The outer boundary of the heliosphere was assumed to be at $R_b = 100$ AU for all calculations except one where $R_b$ was shifted to 150 AU. At this boundary a local interstellar proton spectrum was specified according to Webber, Potgieter, & Burger (1990). Although a termination shock and a subsonic solar wind region inside $R_b$ are expected, it was neglected in our calculations which did not extend below 1 GeV. It is not expected that the inclusion of these structures would have much influence on such relatively high energy cosmic rays (Kóta 1990).

By applying standard perturbation analysis to the focused transport equation containing Eq. (12) we derived a modified focused transport equation that models how energetic particles respond in a statistical average sense on large spatial scales to both the non-uniform solar wind flow and interplanetary magnetic field, and to a collection of dynamic SMFRs advected with the solar wind flow (le Roux et al., 2015, 2018). The derivation was done assuming that SMFR dynamics occur mainly in a 2D plane perpendicular to the guide field (axial) component $B_0$ of the SMFR assuming a strong guide field (J. Birn, 1989; Dmitruk et al., 2004a). The 2D plane contains the magnetic island (twist) component $B_I$ of the SMFR and the SMFR flow $U_I$ determining contraction or merging of the magnetic island. In the inner heliosphere, it appears that assuming a strong guide field so that $B_I / B_0 \ll 1$ is reasonable (Smith et al., 2006). For simplicity, no distinction is made in the derivation between the guide field and the background magnetic field. This approach has some support from observational evidence that the SMFR guide field is aligned with the solar wind spiral magnetic field (Zheng and Hu, 2018). The modified focused transport equation is presented compactly as $$
