\frac {\partial U}{\partial t} + \frac {1}{r ^ {\nu}} \frac {\partial}{\partial r} (r ^ {\nu} V U) - \frac {1}{3 r ^ {\nu}} \frac {\partial}{\partial r} (r ^ {\nu} V) \frac {\partial}{\partial T} (a T U) - \frac {1}{r ^ {\nu}} \frac {\partial}{\partial r} \left(r ^ {\nu} \kappa \frac {\partial U}{\partial r}\right) = 0. \tag {16}
$$ Note that the third term in this equation representing the effect of adiabatic energy changes (Parker 1965, 1966) has arisen quite naturally in this development. The energy changes are associated with expansions and compressions of the cosmic-ray gas due to the geometrical divergence of the solar wind (when $\nu \neq 0$ ), and to the acceleration or deceleration of the solar wind (when $\partial V / \partial r \neq 0$ ). The presence of the latter term emphasizes one of the limitations of the equations, namely, that the characteristic length associated with changes in $V(r)$ should be much larger than the mean free path $(1/4\pi N\sigma)$ in order that a well-defined value of $V$ exists to permit the calculation of $(\delta f/\delta t)_c$ from equation (2). If there are rapid transitions of $V$ and $\kappa$ in a small region $(r,r + \Delta r)$ , the appropriate jump conditions are readily found to be that $U$ and $S$ should be continuous, provided $\Delta r \gg (1/4\pi N\sigma)_{\max}$ ; transitions which take place more rapidly ( $\Delta r < [1/4\pi N\sigma]_{\max}$ ) require a separate treatment. A feature of equation (5) is that the terms involving $f_{0}$ are formally equivalent to those found in the collisionless Boltzmann equation with a radial electric field $E(\boldsymbol{r})$ , namely: $$
v \frac {\partial f _ {0}}{\partial r} + \frac {Z e E (r)}{m (1 - v ^ {2} / c ^ {2})} \frac {\partial}{\partial v} [ (1 - v ^ {2} / c ^ {2}) ^ {5 / 2} f _ {0} ] = 0, \tag {17}
$$ where Ze is the particle charge. The equivalence of terms holds provided $$
