$$ where $R = mv/Ze(1 - v^{2}/c^{2})^{1/2}$ is the particle rigidity; that is, provided $\kappa \propto Rv$ at fixed r. There is indeed evidence that $\kappa$ does have this form in the interplanetary medium (Gloeckler and Jokipii 1966). We see from equation (5) (taking $\partial f_{1}/\partial t = 0$ ) that with $\kappa \propto Rv$ a heliocentric “electric field” model is a good approximation provided $4\pi Nvf_{1}$ is much smaller than the terms involving $f_{0}$ . This is likely to be so at sufficiently high energies where the Reynolds number— $(V/\kappa) \times$ (characteristic length)—is small. Hence there is some theoretical basis for the observation that, except at low energies, the solar modulation of galactic cosmic rays is rather well represented by a single parameter which can be interpreted as being the potential at Earth corresponding to a hypothetical heliocentric “electric field” (Freier and Waddington 1965). Note that the electric field has opposite signs for positively and negatively charged particles, and hence all particles are modulated in phase. The spatial gradient of galactic cosmic rays under quasi-steady conditions $$
G (r, T) = \frac {\partial}{\partial r} (\log f _ {0}) = \frac {\partial}{\partial r} (\log U) = \frac {\partial}{\partial r} (\log j _ {0}), \tag {19}
$$ can be obtained directly from equations (5) or (12). For example, in terms of $j_{0}$ , U, and S, $$
G = \frac {V}{3 \kappa} \left(2 - a T \frac {\partial \log j _ {0}}{\partial T} - \frac {3 S}{V U}\right). \tag {20}
$$ This implies that the gradient depends on both the current density and the cosmic-ray spectrum at the point of observation, in contrast with the result $G = V/\kappa$ obtained from the simple diffusion theory which neglects energy changes (O'Gallagher and Simpson 1967). Rewriting the last term as $\xi(v/V)$ , we see that at high energies where $(v/V) \approx 10^{3}$ a radial anisotropy of 0.1 per cent would be significant. In fact the absence of any detectable radial component of the diurnal variation of the galactic cosmic-ray intensity (McCracken 1965), suggests that $\xi$ and S are quite small at energies of the order of 1–10 GeV and hence that $G\kappa/V$ can be estimated quite accurately for this energy range from the observed spectrum. It is important to note that G is essentially a “differential” gradient referring only to a given particle energy. The integral gradient is less easily obtained, and hence care must be taken in the interpretation of observations made with detectors having a wide (and possibly variable) energy response. This work was supported by the National Aeronautics and Space Administration under grant NASA NsG 382.

